\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background and Context}

On a large residential campus, day-to-day coordination between students still happens through fragmented,
informal channels -- batch WhatsApp groups, hostel groups and word of mouth. This works only when a
student already knows the right people: if nobody in a friend circle is free for a gym session, or headed the
same way for a cab, the plan simply does not happen, even though someone else on campus, statistically,
probably wants the exact same thing. Sharing class notes suffers from the same limitation, and there is
no lasting record of whether someone follows through on a plan once one is made.

CampusLink is proposed as a web-based goal-matching and resource-sharing platform for college campuses.
It reframes an activity that needs another person as a \emph{goal}: a student posts it, the goal appears on a
public campus feed, interested students request to join, and once enough people are in, a small lobby forms
and the group coordinates from there. The same post \arrow{} request \arrow{} fulfil loop is reused for a
smaller, related use case -- academic notes and resource sharing -- where a responder reaches out privately
instead of joining a group.

\subsection{Problem Statement}

Everyday coordination on campus has a few recurring pain points that CampusLink is designed to address:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \textbf{Limited reach:} if a student does not personally know someone free for a given activity,
    the plan does not happen, even though a match likely exists elsewhere on campus.
  \item \textbf{No structure for stranger interaction:} there is no safe, trust-building layer for meeting
    someone unfamiliar, which matters more for things such as late-night rides or one-on-one meetups.
  \item \textbf{Inefficient notes and resource sharing:} asking in several groups for last semester's notes
    often gets no response, or a response too late to be useful.
  \item \textbf{No accountability:} students flake on plans with no record of it, so the next person has no
    way to judge reliability before committing time to them.
\end{itemize}

\section{Project Objectives}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item \textbf{Objective 1:} Let verified students post and discover ``goals'' -- activities that need a
    companion -- through a public, filterable campus feed.
  \item \textbf{Objective 2:} Provide a structured request-to-join and lobby-formation workflow, so a plan
    becomes a group only once a poster approves the right people (or auto-accepts, by their own rule).
  \item \textbf{Objective 3:} Build a trust and safety layer -- reliability scoring, block/report,
    gender-preference filtering and anonymous posting -- so interacting with a stranger feels workable.
  \item \textbf{Objective 4:} Support real-time coordination through in-lobby chat, and a separate private
    chat for the notes/resource-sharing flow.
  \item \textbf{Objective 5:} Record goal outcomes, no-shows and post-goal ratings so future users can judge
    a poster's reliability before committing.
  \item \textbf{Objective 6:} Offer simple, rule-based match suggestions using shared interests, branch and
    previously joined categories.
  \item \textbf{Objective 7:} Design the platform as a stateless, horizontally scalable three-tier web
    application, deployable on free-tier hosting for a live demo.
\end{enumerate}

These objectives describe the platform as scoped in the project proposal; Chapter~4 revisits which of them
are realised in the current build and which remain future work.

