  \caption{Use Case Diagram of CampusLink}
\end{figure}

\clearpage

\subsection{Sequence Diagram}

Figure~2.2 traces a single goal-matching interaction: the requester opens the feed, the frontend asks the
backend for goals, the backend reads them from the database and returns them for display. When the
requester sends a join request, the backend notifies the poster, and once the poster approves, the requester
is accepted and added to the lobby.

\begin{figure}[h!]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{figures/sequence_diagram.jpg}
  \caption{Sequence Diagram for a CampusLink Goal Request}
\end{figure}

\clearpage

\subsection{Activity Diagram}

Figure~2.3 follows the full lifecycle of a goal across the Poster, Requester and System swim-lanes: a goal
is posted in the \texttt{OPEN} state, discovered on the feed, and requested by an interested student. If
auto-accept is enabled the system accepts automatically; otherwise the poster reviews and approves or
rejects the request. Each accepted request increments the goal's accepted count; once the headcount is
reached the goal is marked \texttt{FULL} and its lobby \texttt{CONFIRMED}, otherwise it stays \texttt{OPEN}.
Both sides then coordinate in the lobby chat, and once the activity happens, the system records whether
everyone showed up -- adjusting reliability scores up or down accordingly -- before marking the goal
\texttt{COMPLETED}.

