\section{Database and Domain Model Design}

The domain model, shown in Figure~2.4, is organised around two service layers -- \texttt{AuthService} and
\texttt{GoalService} -- and the entities they manage.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \textbf{User} -- id, email, name, verification flag, branch, year, gender, role, reliability
    score, goals-completed count and no-show count; exposes \texttt{getProfile()} and
    \texttt{updateProfile()}.
  \item \textbf{OtpToken} -- id, email, code hash, purpose, expiry and attempt count, managed by
    \texttt{AuthService} for signup and login verification.
  \item \textbf{Goal} -- id, category, title, description, date/time, headcount, accepted count,
    anonymous flag, gender preference, auto-accept flag, status and expiry; exposes \texttt{cancel()} and
    \texttt{isFull()}, and is managed end-to-end by \texttt{GoalService}.
  \item \textbf{Request} \emph{(planned)} -- id, status and creation time, linking a user to a goal, with
    \texttt{accept()} and \texttt{reject()} operations.
  \item \textbf{Lobby} \emph{(planned)} -- id and status, formed once a goal is confirmed, containing
    lobby members and messages.
  \item \textbf{LobbyMember} \emph{(planned)} -- user id and joined-at timestamp, linking users to a lobby.
  \item \textbf{Message} \emph{(planned)} -- body and sent-at timestamp, exchanged within a lobby.
  \item \textbf{Rating} \emph{(planned)} -- score and no-show flag, given by one user to another after a
    goal closes.
  \item \textbf{Report} \emph{(planned)} -- reason and status, filed by one user against another.
  \item \textbf{Block} \emph{(planned)} -- creation timestamp, recording that one user has blocked another.
\end{itemize}

