\chapter{Conclusion}

\section{Summary of Work}

CampusLink combines goal-based companion matching, a trust-and-safety layer and real-time coordination in
a layered three-tier web platform. The core workflow forms a continuous loop -- post, discover, request,
form a lobby, coordinate, close and rate -- and the same loop is reused, with a private chat in place of a
public lobby, for academic notes and resource sharing. The current build already carries students through
authentication, profile management and feed discovery; the request/lobby state machine and the
trust-and-safety actions are fully specified in the design (Figures 2.1--2.4) as the next slice of work.

\section{Key Findings}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item A public feed combined with a request/approve step turns an unstructured, WhatsApp-group problem
    into an auditable, trackable workflow.
  \item Reliability scoring and no-show flagging give a requester a trust signal about a stranger before
    they commit time to joining that person's plan.
  \item Anonymous posting only works safely with a pre-/post-acceptance boundary: identity stays hidden on
    the public feed but is revealed to a requester once accepted into the lobby.
  \item Reusing the same post/request/fulfil state machine for notes-sharing avoids building and
    maintaining a second workflow for what is functionally the same problem.
  \item Keeping the matching ``intelligence'' rule-based -- shared branch, interests and past categories --
    keeps the system explainable and scoped to what a single-semester course project can engineer properly.
  \item The authentication and feed loop are demonstrably functional in the current build; the request,
    lobby, chat and trust-and-safety layers remain to be implemented (Section~4.3).
\end{itemize}

\section{Future Work and Recommendations}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item Implement the request/lobby state machine shown in Figure~2.3, including auto-accept, manual
    approval/rejection and headcount tracking.
  \item Build the Socket.IO real-time layer for in-lobby chat and the private notes-request chat.
  \item Implement report/block, the gender-preference filter, and the anonymous
    pre-/post-acceptance visibility rule described in Section~2.5.4.
  \item Add no-show flagging and the post-goal rating flow that feeds the reliability score already shown
    on the profile page.
  \item Build the rule-based match-suggestion engine (shared branch, interests, past categories) and
    hostel/proximity-based matching.
  \item Finalise the relational schema shown in Figure~2.4 against the implemented database migrations.
  \item Add continuous integration (build and lint on every push) and deploy the frontend and backend for a
    live, shareable demo.
  \item Run the planned internal pilot (15--20 students) to measure lobby fulfilment rate and no-show rate
    against real usage.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Final Remarks}

CampusLink is built around the idea that campus coordination should not depend on already knowing the
right people. By pairing a simple, auditable post/request/lobby loop with a trust-and-safety layer built
in from the start -- rather than added on afterwards -- the platform aims to make stranger-to-stranger
coordination on campus feel workable rather than risky. The working authentication and feed loop shown in
this report is the foundation that the request/lobby, chat and trust-and-safety layers are designed to
build on next.

